ROWS = [
    dict(id="T212", d="STAT", t="The computational gap for tensor principal component analysis",
 s="Fix an integer k>=3. Observe Y=lambda sqrt(N) theta^tensor(k)+G, where x is uniform on {−1,+1}^N, theta=x/sqrt(N), and G is the symmetrization of a tensor of independent standard Gaussian entries. Prove the conjectured computational lower bound: when lambda is below N^((k−2)/4) by a fixed polynomial factor, no randomized polynomial-time estimator weakly recovers x, meaning correlation bounded away from zero, with absolute correlation used for even k.",
 c="The normalization of the signal and noise is essential. Information-theoretic weak recovery is possible at a constant signal strength, whereas the conjectured computational scale is polynomial in N. Lower bounds for low-degree polynomials, sum of squares, statistical queries or specified Markov chains are not lower bounds for every polynomial-time algorithm. T072 concerns a graph-based planted-clique problem, not this Gaussian tensor observation model.",
 src="A. Das and T. Schramm, Averaged Annealed Markov Chains Achieve the Algorithmic Threshold for Tensor PCA, Part I: Glauber Dynamics, arXiv:2610.16705 (2026), Section 1",
 loc="Introduction, PDF p.4, equation (1) and its following paragraph: 'While this longstanding conjecture remains open'. The paragraph defines the statistical-computational gap and the N^((k−2)/4) scale.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16705",
 why="Tensor PCA is a canonical example of an inference problem that is statistically solvable but apparently computationally inaccessible. A general algorithm below the conjectured scale, or a convincing hardness theory at that scale, would reshape the interpretation of computational barriers in high-dimensional probability.",
 st="Das and Schramm obtain polynomial-time strong recovery at the established algorithmic scale using annealing and ergodic averaging of Glauber dynamics. This closes a proposed gap between local and global methods, while their Introduction explicitly retains the main statistical-computational gap. New 2026 low-degree bounds and average-case reductions address restricted computational models or conditional hardness. Open.",
 n="Fame: the opened source calls tensor PCA a canonical planted problem and documents its extensive probability, spin-glass, statistics and average-case-complexity literature since Richard–Montanari."),
    dict(id='T213',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Quantum capacity of the depolarizing channel: quantum information theory.',
 d='Q',
 t='The quantum capacity of the qubit depolarizing channel',
 s='Determine the unassisted quantum capacity Q(D_p) of D_p(rho)=(1−3p)rho+p(X rho X+Y rho Y+Z rho Z), where X,Y,Z are the Pauli matrices and 0<=p<=1/3. In particular, determine the exact noise threshold at which its quantum capacity becomes zero.',
 c='The parameter p is the probability of each nonidentity Pauli error; the total error probability is 3p. Quantum capacity concerns faithful transmission of unknown quantum states without entanglement assistance. The classical capacity, entanglement-assisted capacity, and performance threshold of a particular quantum code are different quantities. A coherent-information lower bound from one input state is not a capacity formula.',
 src='A. Krohn-Grimberghe, A certified lower bound on the quantum-capacity threshold of the depolarizing channel, arXiv:2608.15870v2 (2026), Sections 2, 7 and 8',
 loc="Section 7, PDF p.4: 'No family-wide or all-state statement, and no capacity formula.' Section 8 states that the gap to the no-cloning ceiling remains large; Section 2 fixes the channel convention.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15870',
 why='This is the simplest symmetric quantum noise model whose capacity still resists an explicit determination. Its obstruction is genuinely probabilistic and information-theoretic: correlated inputs across many uses can outperform single-use coding, making the regularization essential.',
 st='The opened August 2026 paper certifies positive coherent information at per-Pauli p=16239/250000=0.064956 using an explicit 45-copy input. Antidegradability gives zero quantum capacity for p>=1/12. The exact capacity and threshold between these regimes remain undetermined. The September 2026 third-order classical-capacity result arXiv:2609.37240 concerns classical communication and does not close this quantum-capacity gap. Open.',
 n='Fame: a standard foundational quantum-information capacity problem studied since the 1990s; the current paper traces three decades of depolarizing-channel threshold bounds.'),
    dict(id="T214", x="SOLVED (claimed, unrefereed), 14 Nov 2026: the OpenAI math release preprint 'The sharp factor-of-IID threshold for the free Ising model on regular trees' (26 Sep 2026, github.com/openai/math, Thm 1.1) claims the free Ising measure is an Aut(T_d)-equivariant factor of IID for every d >= 3 and tanh(beta) <= (d-1)^(-1/2), equality included; Lean files are supplied. AI-written and unrefereed; this records the claim, not a check of the proof. Replacement row to be screened.", d="TREE", t="Is the free Ising measure on a regular tree a factor of IID up to reconstruction?",
 s="For the infinite d-regular tree, d>=3, let mu_beta be the zero-field ferromagnetic Ising Gibbs measure with free boundary condition. Is mu_beta an Aut(T_d)-equivariant measurable factor of independent identically distributed labels whenever tanh(beta)<=(d−1)^(−1/2)?",
 c="The factor must commute with every tree automorphism. Equivalently, mu_beta is the fair-root binary broadcast process with adjacent-spin correlation tanh(beta). Above the reconstruction threshold it is not a factor of IID. Below that threshold, extremality or a trivial tail is not already a factor construction. T067 asks whether Kesten–Stigum is sharp for reconstruction; here the binary reconstruction threshold is known, and the issue is equivariant generation of the entire process.",
 src="D. Nam, A. Sly and L. Zhang, Ising model on trees and factors of IID, arXiv:2012.09484v2 (2022), Introduction and Theorem 1; R. Lyons, Factors of IID on Trees, arXiv:1401.4197",
 loc="Nam–Sly–Zhang, PDF p.2: 'we conjecture that the Ising model on T should be a factor of IID'; the sentence specifies every d>=3 and tanh(beta)<=(d−1)^(−1/2).",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.09484",
 why="The question separates statistical nonreconstruction from generation by a local invariant rule. Factors of IID describe probabilistic processes compatible with symmetry and local algorithms on large sparse graphs, so identifying their limits is a central problem in probability on trees.",
 st="The uniqueness range tanh(beta)<=1/(d−1) is known. Nam, Sly and Zhang extend the construction to tanh(beta)<=c/sqrt(d−1) for a universal c>0 and sufficiently large d, and explicitly conjecture the full nonreconstruction range. Later Ising factor results found for amenable graphs do not settle the nonamenable regular-tree gap. Open.",
 n="Fame: a question highlighted in Lyons's survey of factors of IID, with a substantial probability paper devoted to extending the known regime."),
    dict(id="T215", d="SM", t="The universal critical slope of the random copolymer at a selective interface",
 s="Determine the exact weak-coupling critical slope m_(1/2)=lim_(lambda down to 0) h_c(lambda)/lambda for the standard disordered copolymer with simple-random-walk excursions. In the normalization Z_N=E exp(−2 lambda sum_(i=1)^N Delta_i(omega_i+h)), the omega_i are independent mean-zero variance-one Gaussian charges, Delta_i is constant on each walk excursion and independently chooses its side with probability 1/2, F=lim N^(−1)E log Z_N, and h_c(lambda)=sup{h:F(lambda,h)>0}.",
 c="The excursion renewal exponent is alpha=1/2. The existence and universality of the slope are known; its exact value is the target. Some papers shift the annealed critical curve and consequently use 1−h_c(lambda)/lambda instead, so their notation must be translated. Monthus's proposed value 2/3 is not the current conjectured answer: its equality prediction has been ruled out. Pinning at renewal contacts is a different disorder model.",
 src="F. Caravenna, G. Giacomin and F. L. Toninelli, Copolymers at selective interfaces: settled issues and open problems, arXiv:1010.5587, Sections 1.2–1.4; Q. Berger, F. Caravenna, J. Poisat, R. Sun and N. Zygouras, arXiv:1301.5308",
 loc="The survey, PDF p.9: 'capturing the value of mα, for α ∈(0,1), is an important open problem.' Equations (4)–(6), (14) and Theorem 1 fix the model, critical curve and slope.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.5587",
 why="The slope is a universal quenched critical quantity for a basic disordered-polymer model. It is a particularly clean test of whether an exact prediction can be extracted from the energy–entropy competition at a random interface.",
 st="The continuum weak-coupling limit proves that the slope exists and depends only on the renewal exponent. The opened later paper arXiv:1301.5308 records strictness of the Monthus lower bound and resolves the finite-mean alpha>1 slope, while retaining the distinct alpha=1/2 problem. Newer copolymer work located in the status searches treats correlated disorder, a reduced model, or other geometries rather than giving this exact slope. Open.",
 n="Fame: Bolthausen–den Hollander's continuum copolymer programme and the Caravenna–Giacomin–Toninelli survey identify this as a principal open problem, with longstanding competing physical predictions."),
    dict(id='T216',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Global solutions of the inhomogeneous Boltzmann equation: partial differential equations.',
 d='SM',
 t='Global strong solutions of the spatially inhomogeneous Boltzmann equation for general large data',
 s='For the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation d_t F+v·grad_x F=Q(F,F) on T^3_x times R^3_v, prove global existence and uniqueness of a classical solution for arbitrary smooth strictly positive initial densities with finite mass, energy and entropy and sufficiently rapid velocity decay, without a smallness or near-equilibrium assumption.',
 c='This is the spatially inhomogeneous, three-dimensional equation. Global renormalized solutions do not supply the uniqueness and regularity demanded here. Global homogeneous theory, near-Maxwellian theory, and one-dimensional models are not the general large-data result. T065 concerns deriving macroscopic hydrodynamics from deterministic particles; the present problem is global well-posedness of the kinetic probability-density equation itself.',
 src='L. He, J.-C. Jiang, J.-I. Kim, D. Lee and S. Park, A Kinetic Concentration criterion for Large-data Boltzmann Dynamics, arXiv:2610.12093 (2026), Introduction',
 loc="Introduction, PDF p.4: 'global well-posedness in a strong or mild class, uniqueness, and unconditional convergence to equilibrium'; the sentence identifies these as central open problems for general large data.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12093',
 why='The distribution of a collisional gas should remain a well-defined density under its own nonlinear kinetic evolution. Establishing this beyond perturbative regimes is a foundational question linking probability, collision geometry and the kinetic explanation of thermodynamics.',
 st='DiPerna–Lions theory gives global renormalized solutions, and perturbative theories give classical solutions near equilibrium. The opened October 2026 paper proves uniqueness, weighted bounds and convergence for global mild solutions satisfying an additional uniform velocity-tail criterion; it explicitly retains the unconditional large-data problem. Recent non-cutoff local classical and one-dimensional large-data results likewise do not resolve this three-dimensional target. Open.',
 n='Fame: a classical central problem of kinetic theory, explicitly identified as such in the opened current paper and situated between the landmark weak-solution and perturbative theories.'),
    dict(id="T217", d="RW", t="Monotonicity of the speed of once-excited random walk in low dimensions",
 s="Consider once-excited nearest-neighbour random walk on Z^d. On the first departure from a site it steps in directions +e_1 and −e_1 with probabilities (1+beta)/(2d) and (1−beta)/(2d), and in each other coordinate direction with probability 1/(2d); on later departures it is simple symmetric. Is its limiting speed v_d(beta)=lim X_n·e_1/n nondecreasing in beta throughout [0,1], for each 2<=d<=8?",
 c="There is one directional cookie per site. Higher excitement changes the subsequently visited set, so a direct ordered coupling of successive steps is not available. One-dimensional multi-cookie monotonicity is a different theorem. T048 concerns a biased walk on a random Galton–Watson tree; here the graph is deterministic and the memory is carried by visited sites.",
 src="R. van der Hofstad and M. Holmes, Monotonicity for excited random walk in high dimensions, arXiv:0803.1881; E. Kosygina and M. P. W. Zerner, Excited random walks: results, methods, open problems, arXiv:1204.1895, Remark 4.11; C.-D. Pham, arXiv:1501.04499",
 loc="Kosygina–Zerner, Remark4.11, PDF p.20: 'monotonically increasing in the drift parameter p if d ≥9'. Pham, PDF pp.1–3, defines the beta normalization and separates smoothness from monotonicity.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1895",
 why="A stronger local directional preference need not transparently produce greater asymptotic speed in a self-interacting process. This basic test of monotonicity links geometric exploration of the range to regeneration and perturbation methods.",
 st="Van der Hofstad and Holmes prove strict monotonicity in all dimensions d>=9, and on a small-bias interval in dimension 8. Pham proves smoothness for positive beta in every d>=2 and further partial monotonicity results for many-cookie variants; these do not establish the full once-excited low-dimensional assertion. The 2026 excited-walk shape results found in the searches concern different radial excitation rules. Open.",
 n="Fame: a standard speed question for the Benjamini–Wilson model, highlighted in the principal excited-walk survey and the subject of several dedicated monotonicity papers."),
    dict(id="T218", d="RW", t="Almost-sure finite-range localization of linearly vertex-reinforced random walk",
 s="Let G be an infinite connected graph of bounded degree. Starting at a fixed vertex, define Z_n(y)=1+sum_(j=0)^n 1{X_j=y}, and choose the next neighbour y of X_n with probability Z_n(y)/sum_(z adjacent to X_n) Z_n(z). Must the set of all visited vertices be finite almost surely? In particular, settle this for the usual nearest-neighbour graph Z^d, d>=2.",
 c="The reinforcement is linear in visits to the destination vertex, with initial weight one. Neither the continuous-time vertex-reinforced jump process nor reciprocally summable superlinear weights are the same model. T047 concerns reinforcement of an edge once after its first traversal; that transition rule does not include this vertex-local-time process as a special case.",
 src="S. Volkov, Vertex-reinforced random walk on arbitrary graphs, arXiv:math/9907196 (1999), abstract; P. Tarrès, Localization of reinforced random walks, arXiv:1103.5536",
 loc="Volkov's original arXiv abstract: 'We conjecture that on all graphs of bounded degree, this happens a.s.' The preceding clause identifies visiting only finitely many vertices as the event.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9907196",
 why="Linear reinforcement balances escape into an infinite graph against the attraction of accumulated local time. Proving finite trapping on a multidimensional lattice would resolve one of the basic questions distinguishing reinforced motion from ordinary random walk.",
 st="Volkov proves finite-range localization with positive probability on a broad class of graphs and almost surely on bounded-degree trees. The one-dimensional linear model has its much sharper five-site theorem. Cotar and Thacker, arXiv:1509.00807, prove eventual two-vertex trapping under superlinear summability assumptions, which exclude linear weights. New continuous-time, interacting-walk and tree-indexed models found in 2023–2026 do not settle the linear discrete-time conjecture. Open.",
 n="Fame: a longstanding Pemantle–Volkov/Tarrès reinforced-walk question, presented in the dedicated localization survey and explicitly conjectured in the original general-graph paper."),
    dict(id="T219", d="SM", t="The cutoff-free three-dimensional Coulomb mean-field limit",
 s="Let N repelling particles in R^3 satisfy d_t X_i=V_i and d_t V_i=N^(−1)sum_(j not i) K(X_i−X_j), with the exact Coulomb force K(x)=x/|x|^3 and independent initial phase points of smooth compactly supported probability density f_0. Prove that, for each fixed T, their empirical phase-space measures converge in probability, uniformly for 0<=t<=T, to the classical solution of d_t f+v·grad_x f+(K*rho_f)·grad_v f=0, rho_f(x)=integral f(x,v)dv, without any microscopic regularization.",
 c="The target is second-order Newtonian motion with no noise and no N-dependent cutoff. Results for first-order Coulomb gradient flows, for a linearization around equilibrium, or for stochastic Vlasov–Poisson–Fokker–Planck dynamics are different. T065 asks for a hydrodynamic limit; this is instead propagation of chaos toward a kinetic equation at fixed macroscopic time.",
 src="M. Feistl-Held and P. Pickl, On the mean-field limit for the Vlasov-Poisson system, arXiv:2504.01471 (2025), Section1.1; M. Duerinckx and P.-E. Jabin, arXiv:2608.04104 (2026)",
 loc="Feistl-Held–Pickl, Section1.1, PDF p.2: the discussion of microscopic derivation without a cutoff says it 'is still an open problem.' Equations(1.1)–(1.2) specify Newtonian dynamics and the Vlasov–Poisson equation.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01471",
 why="The Coulomb singularity creates rare, very close encounters that must be controlled probabilistically without changing the physical interaction. A solution would derive collisionless plasma kinetics directly from the unmodified many-particle dynamics.",
 st="Feistl-Held and Pickl allow a cutoff of order N^(−5/12+sigma). Duerinckx and Jabin (August2026) remove the cutoff for the two-dimensional Coulomb problem and forces locally in L^(2−eta); the three-dimensional Coulomb force is more singular and is not included. September2026 cutoff-free three-dimensional VPFP work adds velocity noise. First-order Coulomb flow and equilibrium-fluctuation advances also leave this nonlinear inertial deterministic problem unresolved. Open.",
 n="Fame: a classical central mean-field-limit problem for Coulomb/Newton interactions, highlighted in the opened current derivation papers and the propagation-of-chaos programme."),
    dict(id="T220", d="SM", t="The critical free-energy asymptotics of marginal disordered pinning",
 s="Let tau be a renewal process starting at 0 with interarrival law K(n)=n^(-3/2)/zeta(3/2), and let omega_n be independent standard Gaussians. Set Z_N(beta,h)=E_tau[exp(sum_(n=1)^N (beta omega_n+h-beta^2/2) 1_{n in tau}) 1_{N in tau}], F(beta,h)=lim_(N to infinity) N^(-1) E_omega log Z_N(beta,h), and h_c(beta)=inf{h:F(beta,h)>0}. For fixed beta>0, determine the sharp asymptotic order of F(beta,h_c(beta)+delta) as delta decreases to zero, including whether the transition has a finite critical exponent or is of infinite order.",
 c="This is the marginal renewal exponent alpha=1/2 with IID disorder and beta held fixed. The weak-disorder critical-point shift h_c(beta) as beta tends to zero, and the scaling limit in an intermediate-disorder critical window, are different questions. A quadratic smoothing upper bound is not a matching asymptotic. T215 concerns a copolymer whose disorder rewards entire excursions rather than renewal contacts; T177 concerns a hierarchical recursive model.",
 src="Q. Berger and H. Lacoin, Pinning on a defect line: characterization of marginal disorder relevance and sharp asymptotics for the critical point shift, arXiv:1503.07315v2 (2015), Section 2.5",
 loc="Section 2.5, 'Open questions', PDF p.6, immediately after (2.22): 'there is no general agreement on what the critical exponent should be for the disordered system as soon as hc(β) > 0.' The preceding sentence singles out alpha=1/2; (2.1)-(2.5) define the model.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07315",
 why="Disordered pinning is the standard renewal model for the effect of quenched randomness on a phase transition. In the marginal Harris case, the critical point is understood much more precisely than the singularity at that point. Determining the singularity would distinguish competing finite-order and infinite-order pictures of disorder smoothing.",
 st="Berger and Lacoin settle the marginal critical-point shift and give its weak-disorder asymptotics, while explicitly leaving this free-energy question open. The smoothing inequality gives F(beta,h_c+delta)<=3 delta^2/(4 beta^2). Wei and Yu's critical disordered pinning measure, arXiv:2402.17642v3, concerns intermediate disorder and does not compute the fixed-beta singularity. The 2024 localized-phase regularity and fluctuation results likewise hold strictly above criticality. Open.",
 n="Fame: the Harris disorder-relevance programme for pinning, developed in standard monographs and surveys; the source devotes its open-questions section to this missing critical behavior."),
    dict(id="T221", d="DYN", t="Rokhlin entropy of Bernoulli shifts over arbitrary countable groups",
 s="For every countably infinite group G and every standard probability space (L,lambda), is h_Rok(G acting on (L^G,lambda^G))=H(L,lambda)? Here G acts by the Bernoulli shift, H is the Shannon entropy of the base, taken as infinity for a base without countable support, and h_Rok is the infimum of the Shannon entropies of countable generating partitions of this ergodic probability-preserving action.",
 c="The conjecture, called RBS by Seward, includes infinite base entropy. The inequality h_Rok<=H is immediate from the coordinate partition; the opposite inequality is the issue. The equality is known for sofic groups. The existence of a non-sofic group does not itself prove either a positive or a negative answer. Isomorphism of Bernoulli shifts with unequal base entropies is a consequence-level question, not a second row here. T034 and T092 concern mixing and spectrum of a single transformation.",
 src="B. Seward, Krieger's finite generator theorem for actions of countable groups II, arXiv:1501.03367v4 (2019), Introduction, statement RBS and Theorem 1.12",
 loc="Introduction, PDF p.3, displayed statement RBS: equality of Rokhlin entropy for the Bernoulli shift and Shannon entropy for the base. The following sentence says it 'may be true for all countably infinite groups G.' Definitions are on PDF pp.1-2.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03367",
 why="Bernoulli shifts are the basic probability-preserving systems of independent random labels. This asks whether independence has its expected intrinsic entropy even when no finite approximation of the acting group is available. A positive answer would preserve the information content of IID sources under every measurable equivariant recoding.",
 st="Seward proves the equality conditionally on positive Rokhlin entropy actions existing for every countably infinite group and establishes it for sofic groups. The inspected 2025-2026 entropy papers concern particular classes of actions or consequences of positive entropy, rather than a proof of RBS for all groups. The 2026 non-sofic-group announcement removes a possible shortcut through universal soficity but does not resolve RBS. Open.",
 n="Fame: a foundational entropy problem in measurable dynamics, posed explicitly alongside the general Bernoulli isomorphism problem in Seward's finite-generator theorem series."),
    dict(id="T222", d="GEOM", t="Steele's variance-limit conjecture for the planar Euclidean traveling salesman problem",
 s="Let X_1,...,X_n be independent uniform points in [0,1]^2 and let L_n be the length of the shortest closed Euclidean tour through them. Prove that Var(L_n) converges to a constant as n tends to infinity, and identify that constant if possible.",
 c="The variance here is unscaled, for the total tour length in the fixed unit square. This is a fluctuation question, distinct from T156, which asks for the constant in the first-order law L_n/sqrt(n). It is not the mean-field traveling salesman model with independent edge costs, and it is not a variance statement about an approximation algorithm. A central limit theorem alone without variance control would not establish the assertion.",
 src="S. Chatterjee, A general method for lower bounds on fluctuations of random variables, arXiv:1706.04290, Section 3, open problem (2)",
 loc="Section 3, PDF p.27, item (2): 'prove that the variance of the length of the optimal tour converges to a constant'. The item specifies uniform points on [0,1]^2 and attributes the conjecture to Mike Steele.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04290",
 why="The Euclidean traveling salesman tour is a basic stochastic optimization functional with a well-understood law of large numbers but an unresolved fluctuation theory. Even convergence of its variance would be a substantial step toward a distributional limit and would test the reach of stabilization methods beyond locally determined functionals.",
 st="The opened source explains that upper fluctuation bounds are well understood and recalls Rhee's lower bound of the correct order in dimension two. It still poses variance convergence explicitly. The inspected recent papers on finite-temperature mean-field optimization, one-dimensional bipartite tours and neural tour solvers do not establish this planar zero-temperature variance limit. No closing theorem was found in the recorded current-status searches. Open.",
 n="Fame: Steele's conjecture in a classical probabilistic optimization model, singled out in Chatterjee's general fluctuation paper and connected there to Steele's standard monograph."),
]
